  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable temperatures, dry weather and an \nincrease in cloudiness is expected this weekend. \n\nThe mid and upper level flow will become more southwesterly and\nsomewhat perturbed over the weekend resulting in increasing \nmoisture and periods of mid and high cloudiness mixed with some \nsunshine. Despite the increasing moisture aloft, dry conditions\nare expected with a stable and dry airmass near the surface. \nA surge of cooler and drier air will continue to spread \nsouthward across central NC this morning behind a cold front \nthat was now pushing into SC and GA. Low level thickness values \nforecast for 12Z this morning will range between 1350 and 1365m \ncompared to the 1380 to 1385m range on Friday morning. This 15 \nto 20m drop in thickness values along with periods of high \ncloudiness will result in highs of 64 north to 71 south which is\nwithin a few degrees of the average high for this time of year.\nWith little change in airmass and conditions, highs on Sunday \nwill be very similar todays. Lows on Saturday and Sunday night \nwill range in the 40s which is near to about 5 degrees above \naverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... While the next best chance of rain will be mid\nto late next week, forecast confidence in specific timing and \namounts remains low.\n\nThe primary driver in our next precipitation chances remains the \nslowly progressive low pressure system over the central CONUS and \nMississippi Valley midweek and how it evolves into the late week \nperiod. Forecast confidence remains below-normal for this time range \nas model guidance varies greatly on a wide variety of solutions that \nwill impact the sensible weather across the Carolinas. \n\nCluster analysis of the leading two solutions from the earlier 12z \nguidance is lining up with the trend within 18z ensemble guidance \nfavoring a northeast lift of low pressure system from the \nMississippi Valley into the Upper Great Lakes region. If this trend \ncontinues and remains void the splitting mid/upper-lvl low scenario, \nforcing for ascent for meaningful precipitation may be extremely \nlimited over the Carolinas. These solutions still do produce light \nrain totals over the Mid-Atlantic and into the Carolinas, but \nreasonable high-end amounts struggle to exceed 0.5\" in 24 hours for \nthe northern/eastern Piedmont and Coastal Plain where moderate to \nextreme drought persists.",
